\section*{Introduction}
Synthesis of Propan-2-yl-methylphosphonofluoridate (PMPF) represents a pivotal milestone in organic chemistry. This compound serves as a model system for studying electrophilic aromatic substitutions and intermolecular interactions.

\section*{Reaction Mechanism}
The reaction proceeds through a Schiff base intermediate, PMPF. This intermediate undergoes nucleophilic attack by the nucleophilic base phosphonate ion (PO4\textsuperscript{3-}). The electrophile replaces a proton from the aromatic ring.

\begin{align*}
R' &\overset{1\text{-ptc}}{}\rightarrow R \\
R' &\overset{2\text{-ptc}}{}\rightarrow R'
\end{align*}

The key parameters affecting selectivity are the identity of the base (e.g., NaH or NaBr) and the molar ratio of the base to the phosphonic acid.

\section*{Safety Protocols}
Synthesis of PMPF requires strict containment. Hydrogen gas generation is prohibited due to explosive risk. All reactions occur in a fume hood equipped with an N2 purge.

\section*{Experimental Details (Lab Notes)}
\begin{itemize}
\item Reagents: 
\begin{itemize}
\item Methyl phosphonate (1.5 mol)
\item Phosphoric acid (1 mol)
\item Water (1 mol)
\end{itemize}
\item Procedure:
\begin{itemize}
\item React under vigorous refluxing conditions.
\item Titrate with NaH.
\item Neutralize with NaOH.
\end{itemize}
\item Observed products:
\begin{itemize}
\item PMPF (1.0 mol)
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

